      "body": "## Overview\n\nReplaces the fixed 6-way split of Quantization Kernels (`parallelism: 6` with `--shard-id`/`--num-shards`) with `automatic_shard`, for the step and its AMD mirror. The planner then sizes the step from main's per-file timings on every build, so it no longer needs a hand-tuned shard count as its tests change.\n\n## Claims\n\n| Job | Before | Plan |\n|---|---|---|\n| `kernels-quantization-test` | 6 fixed shards | 3 shards |\n| its AMD mirror | 6 fixed shards | 3 shards |\n\n- `test_cutlass_scaled_mm.py` and `test_fp8_quant.py` each run in their own pytest command. In one shared process, `test_fp8_quant.py` ran 2.4 times slower after `test_cutlass_scaled_mm.py`, which pushed one shard past 28 minutes.\n- Half the jobs, each a few minutes longer than a fixed shard, and less machine time.\n- Files that skip themselves at module level on a platform (FP8 block, NVFP4, ROCm- or CPU-only kernels) have no timings there; the planner counts a small guess for each, so the plan is conservative.\n\n## Validation\n\n- The sharding harness (each shard's commands run against a mock test tree, as a PR build and as main with the OTel wrapper) shows every test runs exactly once, for both the step and its mirror.\n- `split_check.py` finds no step whose jobs each run the whole suite.\n- Both jobs pass on recent main builds.\n- Build 99747 (scoped, both jobs): every shard passed; slowest about 19.5 minutes on NVIDIA and 12.5 on AMD. Passed counts match main exactly (NVIDIA 3585 across the three commands, AMD 2909); machine time about 23% lower on NVIDIA and 26% lower on AMD. Skipped counts are lower only because a file that skips at module level counts once per pytest process, and the fixed split ran six processes.\n- `/ci run` on this head for the required status.\n\n## Details\n\n- **Labels** drop the `Shard %N` suffix; the generator adds `shard N/M` to sharded jobs.\n- **Timeouts unchanged.** `timeout_in_minutes` stays per job.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n\n\n",
